# 🧠 schtickies Summary — README for Cursor AI

This is a local-first Electron app for Ben. it's called schtickies.

Ben has ADHD. He's the visionary. You are the builder. He will not configure anything manually. You must handle it all.

The goal is to build a minimal, distraction-free interface with sticky notes, automatic summarization, and a floating control panel.

## ✅ Key Concepts

There are 3 types of Schtickies in the app:

---

### 1. Notes Schtickies
- Multiple can be open at once.
- Each sticky is markdown-based.
- do not style markdown yet
- The app must persist which ones were last open.
- They feed content into the Summary Schticky.
- is always yellow background

---

### 2. Summary Schticky
- Read-only.
- Summarizes all open Notes Schtickies.
- Uses the OLAMMA API to summarize content every 30 seconds.
- User can choose the model and directive via Controls Schticky.
- is always blue background

---

### 3. Controls Schticky
- Floating window.
- User can:
  - Choose OLAMMA model
  - Set the summary directive (prompt)
- Changes affect how the Summary is generated.
- always has a "connection" in the form of a straight
- is always grey background

---

## 🎯 Tech & Behavior Requirements

- Use **Electron**
- Style everything using `DANK MONO` font as the style source.
- Persist open notes and settings (model + directive) locally.
- Think of the interface like a graph:
  - Notes are nodes.
  - Summary is the sink node.
  - Controls act like filters.
- Minimalist interface. No toolbars, no clutter.

## 🎯 Spatial Interface Concept

The interface is a physical arrangement of floating windows connected by lines:

```
    [Notes Schticky]──────┐
    (yellow)             │
                         │
    [Notes Schticky]─────┼───→[Summary Schticky]
    (yellow)             │     (blue)
                         │
    [Controls Schticky]──┘
    (grey)
```

Key aspects:
- Each schticky is a separate floating window
- Windows can be moved freely around the screen
- Visual lines connect related schtickies
- Lines update in real-time as windows move
- The arrangement creates a physical "graph" of relationships
- No contained UI - the entire screen is the workspace

